{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210728,"nonce":"TRmISw8VgYW945lp1m0QUWicXBs=","title":"IC kind=1 evict miss must call call_closure_jit_or_sync; bare null returns leak as sentinels","message":"Fixed in commit fd74486. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\nThe recurring `Object does not implement 'split(_)'` (and bare `null` leaks into rendered HTML) flake during navigation on hatch.wrenlift.com is **resolved as of commit `fd74486`** in `src/codegen/runtime_fns.rs::wren_ic_call_inner`.\n\n**Why:** The earlier mitigation (commits `0ada16f`, `ac32d4f`) revalidated the IC's `jit_ptr` against `engine.jit_code[func_id]` but on a stale-or-evict miss returned `Value::null().to_bits()`. The JIT'd caller has no sentinel-vs-real-null distinction, so it'd treat the sentinel as the call's actual return and crash on the next operation against it. That's how `path.split(\"/\")` produced \"null does not implement 'split(_)'\" mid-route.\n\n**Fix shape (live==null branch):** stash the cached closure pointer, clear the IC entry, then re-fetch the VM and call `call_closure_jit_or_sync(vm, closure, \u0026args, None)`. That path picks the right backend (re-JIT if re-tiered, bytecode interpret otherwise) and always returns a real value. The IC clear means subsequent JIT-side visits route through the slow path naturally.\n\n**How to apply:** If a similar JIT IC sentinel-leak shape resurfaces (kind=2/3/6 paths still use cached `jit_ptr` without revalidation today), follow the same pattern: never return `Value::null()` as a \"miss\" sentinel from a path the JIT'd caller treats as a real result. Either refresh and call, or fall through to a real dispatcher (`call_closure_jit_or_sync` / `dispatch_call_rooted`).","files":null}]}